    "move-then-use": {
      "hash": "6a31a72b980d0edc8ead14b624f9c2c71e64bede61bebaa6139c25454f6f4922",
      "note": "",
      "reasons": [
        "unclear"
      ],
      "source": "## What happens when you compile this program? {type=single tags=ownership}\n\n```rust\nfn main() {\n    let s1 = String::from(\"hi\");\n    let s2 = s1;\n    println!(\"{}\", s1);\n}\n```\n\n- [ ] It prints `hi`\n- [x] A compile error: `s1` was moved to `s2`\n- [ ] A compile error: `s2` is never used\n- [ ] It prints `hi` twice\n\n::: explanation\n`String` owns heap data and does not implement `Copy`, so `let s2 = s1;` moves ownership. Using `s1` afterwards is error E0382 (borrow of moved value). Unused variables only produce warnings.\n:::\n",
      "verdict": "fix"
    },
